## Why This Is Asked
Tests expertise in offline-first synchronization, CRDT conflict resolution, and native module design that preserves UI performance. Probes threading, memory, and power trade-offs in a real-world React Native data-sync scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first CRDT data synchronization
- TurboModule/JSI bridge design
- Background thread execution vs UI thread
- Backpressure, tombstone GC, and memory budgeting

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal API sketch
const engine = await init({ peerId: 'device-1', maxLog: 1000 });

engine.onRemoteChange((change) => {
  // Merge remote changes into local state
  console.log('Received:', change);
});

// Apply local drawing operations
engine.applyLocalChange({ type: 'stroke', points: [...], color: '#000' });

// Ensure changes are persisted and synced
await engine.flush();

// Clean up when done
engine.stopSync();
```

## Conflict Resolution
- Last-writer-wins for scalar values; CRDT counters/g-sets for concurrent edits
- Auto-merge without user intervention; conflicts exposed only when semantically impossible to resolve

## Memory Footprint
- Tombstone GC prunes deleted entries above threshold
- Inbox cap (e.g., 1000 pending ops) with spilling to disk
- Lazy loading of historical states

## Battery Impact
- Background thread with coarse-grained intervals (every 5-15s)
- Exponential backoff on failures; WiFi-only mode option
- Backpressure: pause sync when screen off or low battery

