## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-platform native module design, covering model loading, thread management, memory control, and privacy. It probes data marshaling between JS and native layers, lifecycle management, and the ability to handle streaming inference workloads.

## Key Concepts

- TurboModule / JSI integration for high-performance native-JS communication
- Cross-platform backend: TF Lite on Android, Core ML on iOS
- Dedicated worker thread with a ring buffer for input queueing
- Memory budgeting and backpressure via bounded queues
- Privacy-conscious data handling: keep inference on-device, avoid unnecessary data copies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Usage sketch
import { RNMLInference } from 'react-native-ml-infer';

async function runInference() {
  // Initialize the model on a dedicated worker thread
  await RNMLInference.startModel('/models/classifier.tflite', [1, 224, 224, 3], [1, 1000]);

  // Stream input frames and receive results via callback
  RNMLInference.onResult((result) => {
    console.log('Inference result:', result);
  });

  // Feed streaming input (e.g., camera frames)
  for (const frame of cameraStream) {
    await RNMLInference.feedInput(frame);
  }

  // Cleanup when done
  await RNMLInference.stopModel();
}
```

## Thread-Safety & Backpressure

- **Thread Safety**: Use atomic operations or lock-free data structures for the input queue; the worker thread consumes inputs while JS pushes them. Ensure all native object access is guarded by mutexes or serialization queues.
- **Backpressure**: Implement a bounded ring buffer. When the queue is full, either drop the oldest entry or signal back to JS to slow production. This prevents unbounded memory growth.
- **Lifecycle Cleanup**: Invalidate the worker thread on stopModel(); use std::promise/std::future or React Native's native module lifecycle callbacks (invalidate()) to ensure all threads terminate and memory is freed before the module is unloaded.

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses understanding of bridging, threading, backpressure management, and lifecycle handling in a practical logging scenario.

## Key Concepts
- React Native bridge patterns (DirectEvent vs EventEmitter)
- Background thread file tailing with rotation detection
- Fixed-size buffering and batch emission to preserve ordering guarantees
- Backpressure strategies: dropping oldest versus dropping newest
- App lifecycle handling for background task management

## Usage Snippet
```javascript
const Logs = require('react-native-logs');

const logs = Logs.startLogStream({
  logFilePath: '/var/log/app.log',
  pollIntervalMs: 500,
  maxEntriesPerBatch: 100,
});

logs.onLog((entries) => {
  console.log('New log entries:', entries);
});

// Later, when done:
logs.stopLogStream();
```

## Trade-offs
| Strategy | Trade-off |
|----------|------------|
| Drop oldest | Lower memory floor; risk losing historical context |
| Drop newest | Preserve context; may miss recent critical errors |
| File rotation by inode | Reliable on most filesystems; requires platform-specific handling |
| File rotation by size | Simpler to implement; less robust |
| Pause on background | Lower power usage; may miss logs during suspension |
| Background task | Continuous logging; higher battery consumption |

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical RN skills: building reusable components, accessibility across platforms, and theming with Appearance API, plus a small hook pattern.

## Key Concepts

- Accessibility props (accessibilityLabel, accessibilityHint, accessibilityRole)
- Theming with Appearance/useColorScheme and React Context
- Custom hook useTheme and ThemeProvider
- Performance considerations for re-renders and memoization

## Code Example

```javascript
import React, {createContext, useContext, useMemo, useState} from 'react';
import {Pressable, Text} from 'react-native';

export const ThemeContext = createContext('light');
export const useTheme = () => useContext(ThemeContext);

export function ThemeProvider({children}) {
  // Simplified example; in real code use Appearance or useColorScheme
  const [scheme] = useState('light');
  const value = useMemo(() => ({scheme}), [scheme]);
  return <ThemeContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</ThemeContext.Provider>;
}

function Card({title, price}) {
  const {scheme} = useTheme();
  const bg = scheme === 'dark' ? '#222' : '#fff';
  return (
    <Pressable accessibilityLabel={title}
               accessibilityHint="View details"
               accessibilityRole="button"
               style={{backgroundColor: bg}}>
      <Text>{title}</Text>
      <Text>{price}</Text>
    </Pressable>
  );
}
```

